00:00:02.659 --> 00:00:05.090 align:start position:0%
one of the most common measures of pure  athletic performance is vertical jump  how high you can jump off the spot from  a standing position this shows your  explosive potential your ability to  recruit fast twitch muscle fiber however  whilst this is a very good test of  athletic potential it's not actually all  that useful in the real world how often  in sports or in martial arts or in any  other scenario do you need to just leap  off of both feet at once almost always  when we jump or when we kick we're  launching off of one foot and normally  with a run up up to that position so  training your ability to produce force  from one leg is probably more important  than training ability to produce force  through both legs when it comes to  jumping and athleticism not so true when  it comes to lifting things but if we're  talking about athletics and agility then  single leg strength becomes really  important even running involves exerting  force off of alternating legs now of  course you might think you can train  both legs at the same time and that  would be absolutely fine and it would  transfer over to single leg strength so  just squatting in theory should make you  jump higher from a single leg from a  running start and of course this is true  to an extent however it'll only get you  so far and training each leg  independently has a whole bunch of  potential advantages I recently had my  muscle mass scanned got a readout of the  weight and the distribution of muscle  mass across my body I found out that my  left leg was significantly smaller and  lighter than my right leg that was the  only imbalance I had and I suspect that  a lot of people are in the same boat  body builders know the importance of  training specifically for symmetry  that's why they won't just do bench  presses they'll do you know dumbbell  presses as well lots of unilateral  meaning one-armed and lots of bilateral  meaning both arms movements at the same  time to make sure they look the same on  either side otherwise it's very easy for  you to cheat and to help yourself on the  weak side by using more of the strong  side so in a squat you might push more  through the right leg than the left leg  without even realizing it and training  single leg strength will allow you to  overcome that imbalance that way you  know that both legs are as powerful  that'll improve your running speed and  it'll mean that you can jump from either  leg making you significantly quicker  significantly more agile in martial arts  in acrobatics in parkour everything at  the same time of course training single  leg string  this way will help me to balance and  help me to shift your center of gravity  which again is very important for  agility and for all-round performance  but on top of all that we Hill to have  something called the bilateral deficit  to consider the bilateral deficit  basically tells us that the amount of  force we can produce through a single  leg should be greater than the amount of  force we can produce through both legs  by which I mean that using a one-legged  leg press you should be able to produce  more than 60% of your leg press with  both legs  that's discounting things like balance  this wouldn't work for squat but where  all other things are equal you can exert  more strength through a single leg then  you can through both legs at the same  time and the reason for this probably  has to do with neural Drive that is that  when you exert all your force through a  single leg you can concentrate more  effort onto those muscles increase your  muscle fiber recruitment versus trying  to distribute your effort through two  legs so by training again focusing on  single legs at a time  we might be training our neural pathways  in a slightly different way and tapping  into extra strength and performance so  for that reason it's also probably a  good idea to make sure you're  occasionally using unilateral exercises  for your arms for your pecs etc in  general you should be using all your  body sometimes and just parts of your  body sometimes so again there are  benefits to isolation training and  finally the other reason I really like  single leg training and I think you  should incorporate it into your routines  is that it makes it much easier to  increase the challenge particularly with  bodyweight training I've been training a  lot from home lately as anyone who  follows me on Instagram knows and it's  quite difficult to train from home when  you're focusing on legs because you  can't get that same weight most people  don't have a squat rack in their home  most people don't have a barbell that  they can load up with 200 kilograms so  how are you going to create that  challenge at home one way to do this is  through plyometrics explosive jumping  and other moves like that and other ways  by controlling the weight using the kind  of old-time strongmen techniques that  I've talked about in the past and  another way is by using single leg  movements such as the one that gets  squat such as various types of walk and  all these things actually also really  fun ways to train your legs because for  most people training legs is a bit like  eating your vegetables it's the least  exciting thing to do that's especially  true when it comes to body weight but by  doing things on one leg by being  explosive you can actually find that a  leg workout can be quite fun so I have  shared  a full single leg workout routine over  at the Pioneer the link for that of  course we in the description down below  but here are a few exercises that you  can start using right away at home to  start training your single leg strength  as well so the first and most obvious  one is the single legged squat or the  pistol squat the pistol squat of course  means you've also got to keep your heel  on the floor and this is a great way of  training your mobility your flexibility  as well because it's quite tricky to do  that if you don't have full range of  motion at the same time you can of  course add a way to make it more  difficult by holding a kettlebell or by  having a barbell on your shoulders etc  the lunge of course is one of the  best-known single leg exercises and one  that people commonly use and you can  also do a lunch walk so walking around  the garden and in fact any kind of  loaded carry anything where you're  carrying a really heavy weight and just  walking with it is great for your single  leg strength because of course you're  going to be on a single leg at various  point throughout that movement and at  the same time you're engaging your core  and you're balancing I did a whole video  on loaded carries and why they're so  good for you a similar move to the lunge  that I also really like is the side  squat and here you'll basically holding  weight as normal and then you're  stepping out to one side and then  plunging down on that one leg and so  basically it's kind of a cross between a  squat and a lunge that's a great way to  build up to the pistol squat if you  can't do it right away and of course  again you can add more weight if you  want to increase the challenge you've  obviously got your calf raises and  single legged calf raises are almost a  requirement to make that move difficult  enough I've also talked in the past  about calf jumps I really like this  exercise it basically means that you  keep your knees entirely straight and  you launch yourself off of just your  calves so you're really engaging the  curves and the feet in order to exert  that power and I'm gonna be doing my  video on ninja training very soon and  apparently some people think that ninjas  might have used a similar technique in  order to build up their leg strength so  of course you can do calf jumps from a  single leg and you can even make this  another form of travel by doing calf  jumps on one leg all the way down the  garden another really good one you can  do with no weight though is a squat walk  basically you're staying really low to  the ground as though you're squatting  and then walking your way around the  garden and you can combine all these  into a kind of circuit or kind of  mechanical drop set you can get a really  intense workout on your legs wherever  you are using just your bodyweight by  focusing on just the leg at the time  there are ton more exercises of course  that you can use and I'd love you to  share them down in the comments below  and I'll be addressing this topic in the  future soon I do have plans to make a  video on jumping height in general so  I'll be discussing that more then I hope  you found this video useful and  interesting guys if you did then please  leave a like please share it around like  I say please comment down below all that  stuff really helps me out  massively got lots more content on the  way soon I'm gonna be doing a video on  the life of Bruce Lee one of my learning  from legends videos as well as Houdini  I'm gonna be doing ninja training like I  said and lots more ancient warrior  training I'm gonna do a video on Qi  control and what actually is in martial  arts whether it's real whether it's not  etc I'll be doing a video on personality  and how that affects your focus as well  as your physical performance lots more  stuff in the pipeline for the new year  so that all sounds good then please  stick around and I'll see you next time  Merry Christmas and bye for now
 

